at operations are available. But that might not directly indicate faults. \n\nAnother approach: check the pods and deployments to see if any are in a bad state. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the astronomy-shop namespace. That would show the current state of the workloads. If any pods are crashing or deployments are not stable, that\'s a good starting point. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check the pods and deployments. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the specified namespace. This will give an overview of the current state of the application, identifying any pods that are not running or deployments that have issues. \n\nTherefore, the tool to use now is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" and then "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop". But since the tool allows only one command per call, maybe start with getting the pods first. \n\nWait, the user can only make one tool call per step. So first, get the pods. Then, if needed, check deployments. So the next tool call should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop".\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo diagnose potential faults, I need to inspect the current state of pods and deployments in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. This will reveal if any pods are in a non-Running state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error) or if deployments have unexpected replicas, scaling issues, or rollout problems. Starting with `kubectl get pods` provides immediate visibility into active workloads.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 576, 'prompt_tokens': 1735, 'total_tokens': 2311, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfb-128b-7e73-b23d-f44c928d04f4-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1735, 'output_tokens': 576, 'total_tokens': 2311}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c8fcd2e1-a043-475a-9a2a-09384b85b8d9'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call for the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The previous step was to get the list of services using get_services, which gave a list of services in the astronomy-shop namespace. Now, the next logical step is to check the current state of the pods and deployments in that namespace.\n\nThe user mentioned that the application has many microservices, and some might have faults. To start diagnosing, I need to check if any pods are not running properly. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows me to run kubectl commands. The most relevant command here is 'kubectl get pods' to list all pods in the namespace. This will show if any pods are in a CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or other non-Running states. \n\nAdditionally, checking deployments with 'kubectl get deployments' would help see if there are any issues with replica counts or rollout statuses. However, the user's previous step was about services, so focusing on pods first makes sense. The command should include the namespace parameter to ensure we're looking at the correct namespace, 'astronomy-shop'. \n\nSo, the tool call should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'. This will retrieve the pod statuses and help identify any immediate issues. If there are problems, the next steps can involve checking logs, metrics, or dependencies based on the pod statuses.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 