ainer is failing and being restarted.\n\nNow, the next step is to figure out why the container is failing. The logs might have more details. The user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, so they can check the logs of the product-catalog container. Using kubectl logs on the specific pod should show any error messages or exceptions that occurred when the container crashed. That could point to the root cause, like a configuration error, dependency issue, or application crash.\n\nAlternatively, they could check the events in more detail or look into the deployment configuration. But since the container is exiting with code 1, which is a general error, logs are the best next step. So, the tool call should be to get the logs of the product-catalog pod.\n</think>\n\nkubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop  \nThe container exited with status code 1, indicating an error. Examining the logs will reveal the specific cause of the crash (e.g., application errors, dependency failures, or configuration issues). This is critical for identifying the root cause of the restarts.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 360, 'prompt_tokens': 4219, 'total_tokens': 4579, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfb-6c8e-73a1-861a-453bcaa92d20-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4219, 'output_tokens': 360, 'total_tokens': 4579}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ff9c7999-2713-4545-878f-b30727385f14'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the product-catalog pod is restarting. From the previous steps, they checked the pods and found that product-catalog had 2 restarts. Then they described the pod and saw that the container exited with code 1, which is an error. Now they need to check the logs to find out why.\n\nThe next logical step is to get the logs from the product-catalog container. The command would be kubectl logs followed by the pod name and namespace. The user already used kubectl describe, which showed the container exited with code 1. The logs will give more details on what went wrong. \n\nLooking at the available tools, the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd is the right choice. The command should be "kubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop". This will retrieve the logs from the specific pod. \n\nOnce the logs are obtained, they can look for error messages or stack traces that indicate the root cause. Common issues might be configuration errors, missing dependencies, or problems connecting to databases or other services. For example, if the DB_CONNECTION_STRING is incorrect, the app might fail to start. Also, checking if the container is crashing due to a runtime error or an unhandled exception in the application code would be important. \n\nAfter getting the logs, the user might need to check the environment variables, especially DB_CONNECTION_STRING, to ensure the database is accessible. They might also need to look into the product-catalog service\'s dependencies, like the flagd service or the PostgreSQL database, to see if there are any connectivity issues. \n\nIf the logs don\'t show enough, maybe checking the events or using get_traces to see if there are any errors in the service\'s operations could help. But the immediate next step is definitely to get the logs to understand the error message.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs