ainer is failing and being restarted.\n\nNow, the next step is to figure out why the container is failing. The logs might have more details. The user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, so they can check the logs of the product-catalog container. Using kubectl logs on the specific pod should show any error messages or exceptions that occurred when the container crashed. That could point to the root cause, like a configuration error, dependency issue, or application crash.\n\nAlternatively, they could check the events in more detail or look into the deployment configuration. But since the container is exiting with code 1, which is a general error, logs are the best next step. So, the tool call should be to get the logs of the product-catalog pod.\n</think>\n\nkubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop  \nThe container exited with status code 1, indicating an error. Examining the logs will reveal the specific cause of the crash (e.g., application errors, dependency failures, or configuration issues). This is critical for identifying the root cause of the restarts.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 360, 'prompt_tokens': 4219, 'total_tokens': 4579, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfb-6c8e-73a1-861a-453bcaa92d20-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4219, 'output_tokens': 360, 'total_tokens': 4579}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ff9c7999-2713-4545-878f-b30727385f14'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the product-catalog pod is restarting. From the previous steps, they checked the pods and found that product-catalog had 2 restarts. Then they described the pod and saw that the container exited with code 1, which is an error. Now they need to check the logs to find out why.\n\nThe next logical step is to get the logs from the product-catalog container. The command would be kubectl logs followed by the pod name and namespace. The user already used kubectl describe, which showed the container exited with code 1. The logs will give more details on what went wrong. \n\nLooking at the available tools, the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd is the right choice. The command should be "kubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop". This will retrieve the logs from the specific pod. \n\nOnce the logs are obtained, they can look for error messages or stack traces that indicate the root cause. Common issues might be configuration errors, missing dependencies, or problems connecting to databases or other services. For example, if the DB_CONNECTION_STRING is incorrect, the app might fail to start. Also, checking if the container is crashing due to a runtime error or an unhandled exception in the application code would be important. \n\nAfter getting the logs, the user might need to check the environment variables, especially DB_CONNECTION_STRING, to ensure the database is accessible. They might also need to look into the product-catalog service\'s dependencies, like the flagd service or the PostgreSQL database, to see if there are any connectivity issues. \n\nIf the logs don\'t show enough, maybe checking the events or using get_traces to see if there are any errors in the service\'s operations could help. But the immediate next step is definitely to get the logs to understand the error message.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b93e71e86847cbf1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 440, 'prompt_tokens': 5246, 'total_tokens': 5686, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfb-7dc8-7b62-935e-532c1356e863-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs product-catalog-f57789b6d-566nw -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b93e71e86847cbf1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5246, 'output_tokens': 440, 'total_tokens': 5686}), ToolMessage(content='2026/03/17 22:42:56 exporter export timeout: rpc error: code = Unavailable desc = data refused due to high memory usage\n2026/03/17 22:43:31 context deadline exceeded: rpc error: code = DeadlineExceeded desc = context deadline exceeded while waiting for connections to become ready\n2026/03/17 22:43:32 traces export: exporter export timeout: rpc error: code = Unavailable desc = connection error: desc = "transport: Error while dialing: dial tcp 10.107.202.40:4317: connect: connection refused"\n2026/03/17 22:43:41 context deadline exceeded: rpc error: code = DeadlineExceeded desc = context deadline exceeded while waiting for connections to become ready\n2026/03/17 22:43:42 traces export: exporter export timeout: rpc error: code = Unavailable desc = connection error: desc = "transport: Error while dialing: dial tcp 10.107.202.40:4317: connect: connection refused"\n2026/03/17 22:43:51 exporter export timeout: rpc error: code = Unavailable desc = connection error: desc = "transport: Error while dialing: dial tcp 10.107.202.40:4317: i/o timeout"\n', id='04e20de9-e366-4623-9ac4-1a2c56dd1cdf', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b93e71e86847cbf1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b264ebf9-9400-4f58-ad3f-3b31135f73aa'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The product-catalog pod is restarting because of errors related to the OpenTelemetry exporter. The logs show connection refused errors when trying to reach the OTel collector at 10.107.202.40:4317. So the problem is likely that the OpenTelemetry collector isn\'t running or isn\'t accessible.\n\nFirst, I should check if the otel-collector-agent pod is running. The user already ran kubectl get pods and saw that otel-collector-agent-7fmss is Running. But maybe there\'s an issue with its service or network configuration. \n\nWait, the logs mention the OTel collector\'s endpoint as http://$(OTEL_COLLECTOR_NAME):4317. The OTEL_COLLECTOR_NAME is set to "otel-collector", so the service name should be otel-collector. I should check if the service for the OTel collector exists and is correctly configured. \n\nUsing get_services from Grafana might not help here since it\'s about service names in the context of monitoring. Instead, I need to check the Kubernetes services. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used to get the service details for the OTel collector. \n\nSo, the next step is to describe the otel-collector service to see its endpoints and ensure it\'s correctly exposing the port 4317. If the service isn\'t properly set up, the product-catalog can\'t connect to the collector, leading to the connection refused errors. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is misconfigured, or the pod isn\'t reachable. Checking the service\'s YAML and endpoints would clarify. Also, checking the network policies or if there\'s a firewall blocking the port could be necessary, but since the user has limited tools, focusing on Kubernetes resources is better. \n\nTherefore, using kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop would show the service\'s configuration, endpoints, and any issues. If the endpoints are not listed, the service might not be properly pointing to the pod, or the pod isn\'t ready. This would help identify the root cause.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe logs indicate the product-catalog service cannot connect to the OpenTelemetry collector at `otel-collector:4317`. I need to verify if the `otel-collector` service is running and properly exposed. Using `kubectl describe service` will show its status, endpoints, and any misconfigurations preventing connectivity.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 550, 'prompt_tokens': 4795, 'total_tokens': 5345, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfb-96de-7bf2-974d-355a37040309-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4795, 'output_tokens': 550, 'total_tokens': 5345}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b30f8dad-f8c1-4ee4-b97a-92b5a1a2219c'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the product-catalog service is crashing. From the logs, it looks like the issue is with connecting to the OpenTelemetry collector. The error messages mention connection refused and timeouts when trying to reach 10.107.202.40:4317. \n\nFirst, I need to check if the otel-collector service is up and running. The user already tried checking the product-catalog pod's logs and saw that the container is exiting due to connection issues. The next logical step is to verify the status of the otel-collector service.\n\nUsing kubectl describe service otel-collector in the astronomy-shop namespace should show the service's details. If the service isn't running, or if there are no endpoints, that would explain why the product-catalog can't connect. Also, checking the service's YAML configuration to ensure the port and target are correct is important. \n\nWait, the user might also need to check if the otel-collector pod is running. Maybe the service is misconfigured, pointing to a non-existent pod. So, after describing the service, looking at the Endpoints section would tell if there's an IP assigned. If not, the pod might be down. \n\nAdditionally, the service's selector should match the labels of the otel-collector pod. If there's a mismatch, the service won't find any endpoints. The user should check the selector in the service's YAML. \n\nAnother possibility is that the otel-collector is running but the port isn't exposed correctly. The logs mention port 4317, so ensuring the service is listening on that port and that the target port in the service matches the container's port is crucial. \n\nSo, the next step is to describe the otel-collector service to get its status, endpoints, and configuration. That should help identify if the service is misconfigured or if the underlying pod isn't running. If the service is okay, then the issue might be with the otel-collector pod itself, which would require checking its logs or status.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-889d9942adf0da2f', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 475, 'prompt_tokens': 5807, 'total_tokens': 6282, 'completion_tokens_details': N