2026-03-17 22:49:33 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 2] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='34ae66c1-b8d3-4b2b-9f15-bdff6e1fee99'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\n\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nastronomy-shop\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. The main problem seems to be with the product-catalog service, which is restarting repeatedly. From the logs, it looks like the product-catalog can't connect to the OpenTelemetry collector (otel-collector) at 4317, resulting in connection refused errors.\n\nFirst, I checked the pods in the namespace and saw that product-catalog had two restarts. Then, when I described the pod, I saw that the container exited with code 1, and the logs mentioned connection issues to the otel-collector. Next, I checked the otel-collector service and found that it had no endpoints, which means no pods are associated with it. \n\nBut wait, there's a pod named otel-collector-agent-7fmss that's running. So why isn't it being picked up by the service? The service's selector is based on labels. I need to check if the pod's labels match the service's selector. The service's selector is app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. \n\nLooking at the pod's labels, the otel-collector-agent pod has labels like app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. Those should match the service's selector. But the service still has no endpoints. Maybe there's a typo or a misconfiguration in the service's YAML? Or perhaps the pod isn't properly labeled?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is using the wrong port or protocol. The otel-collector pod is listening on port 4317 for OTLP, but the service might be configured with a different port. Wait, in the service description, the otlp port is 4317/TCP, and the endpoints are <none>. So the service is correctly configured to expose port 4317, but no pods are matching the selector. \n\nWait, the pod's name is otel-collector-agent-7fmss, and the service's selector includes component=agent-collector. The pod's labels include component=agent-collector, so that should match. But why no endpoints? Maybe the pod isn't ready yet? But the pod status shows it's Running. Or maybe there's a problem with the ConfigMap or the configuration file that the collector is using, causing it to not start properly? \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is in the wrong namespace? No, the service and pod are both in the astronomy-shop namespace. Hmm. Maybe the service is not properly created, or there's a typo in the selector. Let me check the service's YAML. But since I can't do that directly, I have to rely on the existing data. \n\nGiven that the service has no endpoints, the most likely cause is that the selector isn't matching any pods. Even though the pod's labels seem to match, there might be a discrepancy. For example, maybe the service's selector is using a different key or value. Or perhaps the pod hasn't been properly registered yet. \n\nBut the pod is running and has been for a few minutes. So the issue is probably a misconfiguration in the service's selector. The product-catalog service is trying to connect to otel-collector:4317, but since the service has no endpoints, it can't resolve the IP, leading to connection refused. \n\nTherefore, the root cause is the misconfigured service selector for otel-collector, leading to no endpoints and connection failures. The faulty component is the otel-collector service in the astronomy-shop namespace.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure is that the `otel-collector` service in the `astronomy-shop` namespace has no endpoints, indicating no pods are associated with it. This is likely due to a misconfiguration in the service's selector or the pod's labels not matching the service's requirements. As a result, the `product-catalog` service cannot connect to the OpenTelemetry collector, causing export timeouts and connection refused errors. \n\n**Faulty Component:** The `otel-collector` service is misconfigured or the pod's labels do not match the service's selector, leading to no endpoints and connection failures.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b6b80cf2-abc0-4df4-bc46-b8692525cf2c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a542ba54-9f0d-4ebf-868f-7a7844eb5edb'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by understanding the problem. The user mentioned that the product-catalog service is having issues, specifically connection refused errors to the OpenTelemetry collector at 4317. The initial steps showed that the otel-collector service has no endpoints, which means no pods are associated with it. \n\nFirst, I need to verify the current state of the services and pods. The user has access to several tools. The get_services tool can retrieve service names from Grafana, which might help identify if the otel-collector service is listed. However, since the user already knows the service name, maybe checking the service\'s configuration is more direct.\n\nThe exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool can be used to describe the otel-collector service and its pods. By running \'kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop\', I can check the selector and endpoints. This will show if the service\'s selector matches the pod\'s labels. If there\'s a mismatch, that\'s the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, checking the pods associated with the otel-collector service using \'kubectl get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector -n astronomy-shop\' would confirm if any pods are running. If no pods are found, there might be an issue with the deployment or the pod\'s labels. \n\nWait, the user already mentioned that the otel-collector-agent pod is running. So the service might not be correctly referencing it. The service\'s selector should match the pod\'s labels. If the service\'s selector is using a different label key or value, the endpoints won\'t be listed. \n\nUsing the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to describe the service and check the selector and endpoints would be the next step. That way, I can see if the service is properly configured to point to the existing pod. If the selector is incorrect, adjusting it to match the pod\'s labels would resolve the issue. \n\nAnother possibility is that the service is not exposed correctly, but since the pod is running, the main issue is the selector mismatch. So the first action is to check the service\'s configuration and the pods\' labels to ensure they align.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: To diagnose the issue, I need to check the current state of the `otel-collector` service and its associated pods. By describing the service, I can verify if it has endpoints (i.e., associated pods) and inspect its selector configuration. This will confirm whether the service is properly linked to the running `otel-collector-agent` pod and identify any misconfigurations.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 560, 'prompt_tokens': 2584, 'total_tokens': 3144, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfc-c21d-7fc1-808d-78a08aae00cb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2584, 'output_tokens': 560, 'total_tokens': 3144}), HumanMessage(content='