   Tue, 17 Mar 2026 22:43:36 +0000\n    Ready:          True\n    Restart Count:  0\n    Limits:\n      memory:  200Mi\n    Requests:\n      memory:   200Mi\n    Liveness:   http-get http://:13133/ delay=0s timeout=1s period=10s #success=1 #failure=3\n    Readiness:  http-get http://:13133/ delay=0s timeout=1s period=10s #success=1 #failure=3\n    Environment:\n      MY_POD_IP:       (v1:status.podIP)\n      K8S_NODE_NAME:   (v1:spec.nodeName)\n      K8S_NODE_IP:     (v1:status.hostIP)\n      GOMEMLIMIT:     160MiB\n    Mounts:\n      /conf from opentelemetry-collector-configmap (rw)\n      /hostfs from hostfs (ro)\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-wc6zx (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type                        Status\n  PodReadyToStartContainers   True \n  Initialized                 True \n  Ready                       True \n  ContainersReady             True \n  PodScheduled                True \nVolumes:\n  opentelemetry-collector-configmap:\n    Type:      ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:      otel-collector-agent\n    Optional:  false\n  hostfs:\n    Type:          HostPath (bare host directory volume)\n    Path:          /\n    HostPathType:  \n  kube-api-access-wc6zx:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   Burstable\nNode-Selectors:              <none>\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure:NoSchedule op=Exists\n                             node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure:NoSchedule op=Exists\n                             node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists\n                             node.kubernetes.io/pid-pressure:NoSchedule op=Exists\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule op=Exists\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason     Age    From               Message\n  ----    ------     ----   ----               -------\n  Normal  Scheduled  5m34s  default-scheduler  Successfully assigned astronomy-shop/otel-collector-agent-7fmss to node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Normal  Pulled     5m30s  kubelet            spec.containers{opentelemetry-collector}: Container image "otel/opentelemetry-collector-contrib:0.142.0" already present on machine\n  Normal  Created    5m26s  kubelet            spec.containers{opentelemetry-collector}: Created container: opentelemetry-collector\n  Normal  Started    5m25s  kubelet            spec.containers{opentelemetry-collector}: Started container opentelemetry-collector\n', id='2933fed7-f7cf-4c4d-9d4b-649ea6c68451', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ae9bc7414270bcf3'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='116c0984-6c13-48f7-8bfc-fd3d800cb600'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix an issue where the product-catalog service can\'t connect to the OpenTelemetry collector. From the previous steps, they checked the pod and found that the otel-collector-agent pod is running and has the correct labels. But the service for otel-collector has no endpoints. \n\nSo the next step is to check the service configuration. The user already described the pod, which shows the pod is running and has the right labels. Now they need to verify the service\'s selector and endpoints. The service\'s selector should match the pod\'s labels. The pod\'s labels include app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. \n\nThe user should check the service\'s YAML to see if the selector is correctly set. But since they can\'t directly view the service\'s YAML, maybe they can use kubectl to describe the service. Wait, in the previous step, the user tried to describe the pod, but the service\'s info wasn\'t checked yet. \n\nWait, the user\'s last tool call was to describe the pod, which showed the pod\'s details. Now, they need to check the service. So the next logical step is to describe the otel-collector service to see its configuration, specifically the selector and endpoints. If the service\'s selector doesn\'t match the pod\'s labels, that\'s the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t properly created, or there\'s a typo in the labels. So using kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop would show the service\'s details, including the selector and endpoints. That would confirm if the service is correctly pointing to the pod. \n\nTherefore, the next tool to use is exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command to describe the service. This will help identify if the service\'s selector is misconfigured, leading to no endpoints and the connection issues in product-catalog.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: The `otel-collector` service has no endpoints, meaning no pods are matching its selector. To diagnose this, I need to inspect the service\'s configuration (e.g., selector, ports) and verify if it aligns with the `otel-collector-agent` pod\'s labels. This will confirm whether the service is properly linked to the pod and identify any misconfigurations.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 522, 'prompt_tokens': 4196, 'total_tokens': 4718, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfd-01db-7893-847c-0b242a3c3ef1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4196, 'output_tokens': 522, 'total_tokens': 4718}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='af087090-2384-49d9-bfb3-01831a351d0e'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the product-catalog service can't connect to the OpenTelemetry collector. Earlier, they checked the pod and found that the otel-collector-agent pod is running with the correct labels. But the service otel-collector has no endpoints. So the next step is to check the service's configuration.\n\nThe user just used the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to describe the pod, and now they need to describe the service. The service's selector might not be matching the pod's labels. Let me make sure the service's selector is set to the same labels as the pod. The pod has labels like app.kubernetes.io/instance, app.kubernetes.io/name, component, etc. The service's selector should match these exactly. If there's a typo or a mismatch, the service won't find the pod, leading to no endpoints.\n\nSo, by describing the service, the user can check the selector and other configurations. If the selector is incorrect, they can update it to match the pod's labels. Also, verifying the ports to ensure that the service is exposing the correct port (like 4317 for OTLP) is important. Once the service is correctly configured, the endpoints should appear, allowing the product-catalog to connect successfully.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9c1b142b855152c8', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 309, 'prompt_tokens': 5330, 'total_tokens': 5639, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfd-1abc-7c52-ace6-85d30af86f9a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9c1b142b855152c8', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5330, 'output_tokens': 309, 'total_tokens': 5639}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                     otel-collector\nNamespace:                astronomy-shop\nLabels:                   app.kubernetes.io/component=agent-collector\n                          app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop\n                          app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=Helm\n                          app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector\n                          app.kubernetes.io/part-of=opentelemetry-collector\n                          app.kubernetes.io/version=0.142.0\n                          component=agent-collector\n                          helm.sh/chart=opentelemetry-collector-0.142.2\nAnnotations:              meta.helm.sh/release-name: astronomy-shop\n                          meta.helm.sh/release-namespace: astronomy-shop\nSelector:                 app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop,app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector,component=agent-collector\nType:                     ClusterIP\nIP Family Policy:         SingleStack\nIP Families:              IPv4\nIP:                       10.107.202.40\nIPs:                      10.107.202.40\nPort:                     jaeger-compact  6831/UDP\nTargetPort:               6831/UDP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nPort:                     jaeger-grpc  14250/TCP\nTargetPort:               14250/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nPort:                     jaeger-thrift  14268/TCP\nTargetPort:               14268/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nPort:                     metrics  8888/TCP\nTargetPort:               8888/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nPort:                     otlp  4317/TCP\nTargetPort:               4317/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nPort:                     otlp-http  4318/TCP\nTargetPort:               4318/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nPort:                     zipkin  9411/TCP\nTargetPort:               9411/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nSession Affinity:         None\nInternal Traffic Policy:  Local\nEvents:                   <none>\n', id='7af11d31-8ae6-4996-9b90-0e79675b68cb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9c1b142b855152c8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='715d6bcc-5174-41a7-85de-45ec671a00ee'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with an issue where the product-catalog service in the astronomy-shop namespace can\'t connect to the OpenTelemetry collector (otel-collector). The logs show connection refused errors on port 4317. \n\nFirst, I checked the pods and saw that the otel-collector-agent pod is running, but the otel-collector service has no endpoints. That means no pods are being selected by the service. The service\'s selector is set to app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. \n\nLooking at the pod\'s labels, the otel-collector-agent pod has those same labels. So why isn\'t it matching? Maybe there\'s a typo or a misconfiguration in the service\'s selector. Wait, the service\'s selector includes component=agent-collector, and the pod\'s label is component=agent-collector. That should match. \n\nWait, maybe the service is using the wrong port? The service\'s port for otlp is 4317/TCP, which matches the pod\'s exposed port. But the endpoints are still empty. Could there be a problem with the pod\'s readiness or liveness probes? The pod\'s status shows it\'s running, but maybe the probes are failing, causing the service not to register it. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not properly created, or there\'s a conflict in the labels. Let me check the pod\'s YAML to see if the labels are correctly set. But since I can\'t do that directly, I have to rely on the existing data. \n\nAnother possibility is that the service is in the wrong namespace. But both the service and pod are in the astronomy-shop namespace. Hmm. Maybe the service is not properly associated with the pod because of a version mismatch or a misconfigured Helm chart. \n\nWait, the service\'s selector includes app.kubernetes.io/instance and name, which match the pod\'s labels. So why no endpoints? It\'s possible that the service is not correctly referencing the pod\'s labels. Maybe the service\'s selector is missing a key or has an extra one. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the pod hasn\'t been properly registered yet, but the pod has been running for a few minutes. I should check the pod\'s events to see if there are any issues. But the user hasn\'t provided that info. \n\nGiven the information, the most likely cause is that the service\'s selector is not matching the pod\'s labels, even though they seem to match. Maybe there\'s a typo in the service\'s YAML. To confirm, I should check the service\'s YAML configuration to ensure the selector is correctly set. But since I can\'t do that, I\'ll proceed to check the pod\'s labels again and ensure they match the service\'s selector. \n\nWait, the service\'s selector is app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The pod\'s labels are exactly those. So why no endpoints? Maybe the service is not properly created, or there\'s a typo in the service\'s name. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not properly exposed. But the service type is ClusterIP, which should work. \n\nI think the root cause is that the service\'s selector is not matching the pod\'s labels, even though they appear to match. To resolve this, I need to ensure the service\'s selector is correctly configured to match the pod\'s labels. If the labels are correct, maybe the service needs to be recreated or the pod needs to be restarted. \n\nBut since the user can\'t modify the service\'s YAML directly, maybe the issue is that the service is not properly linked to the pod. The next step is to check the pod\'s labels again and ensure they match the service\'s selector. If they do, then the service should have endpoints. If not, adjust the service\'s selector accordingly.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: The `otel-collector` service has no endpoints despite the `otel-collector-agent` pod being running and having matching labels. This suggests a misconfiguration in the service\'s selector or a failure to register the pod. To resolve this, I need to verify the service\'s YAML configuration (specifically the selector) and ensure it matches the pod\'s labels. If the selector is correct, the service should automatically bind to the pod. If not, the service needs to be updated.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 960, 'prompt_tokens': 4889, 'total_tokens': 5849, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfd-2ce4-7310-beef-fcadf06df130-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4889, 'output_tokens': 960, 'total_tokens': 5849}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='92b23270-a736-47fb-9dd7-07d316cf1973'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the otel-collector service has no endpoints. They checked the pod and the service, and everything seems labeled correctly. But the service isn't picking up the pod. Hmm.\n\nWait, the service's selector is set to app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The pod's labels match those exactly. So why no endpoints? Maybe the service is using the wrong port? Or maybe the pod isn't ready yet? But the pod is running and has been for a while. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is misconfigured in another way. The user tried getting the service in YAML to check. The output shows the service is of type ClusterIP, which is correct. The ports are all set, but endpoints are still none. \n\nWait, maybe the pod's labels are not being properly recognized. Let me check the pod's labels again. The pod has app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The service's selector includes all three. That should match. \n\nIs there a possibility of a typo or a missing label? The pod's labels look correct. Maybe the service is not in the same namespace? No, both are in astronomy-shop. \n\nAnother thought: maybe the service is not properly created. If the service was deleted or not applied correctly, it might not have endpoints. But the user can see the service exists. \n\nWait, the pod is a DaemonSet. DaemonSets ensure a pod runs on each node. But if there's only one node, maybe the pod is running but the service isn't selecting it. Or maybe the service's selector is too specific. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service needs to be updated to include the pod's labels. But the labels match. I'm confused. The user is trying to get the service's YAML to check for any discrepancies. \n\nWait, the user just called kubectl get service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop -o yaml. The response will show the service's full configuration. Let me check that. \n\nIn the previous step, the user got the service's describe output, which showed the selector and no endpoints. Now, getting the YAML might reveal if the service is correctly configured. For example, maybe the service is using the wrong port or has a typo in the selector. \n\nOnce the YAML is retrieved, the user can compare the selector with the pod's labels. If they match, then maybe the issue is with the pod not being ready or the service not being properly reconciled. If there's a discrepancy, the service needs to be updated. \n\nSo the next step is to get the service's YAML to check for any configuration issues. That's why the user called the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command to get the service in YAML format.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-947df208aaa614a1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 653, 'prompt_tokens': 6047, 'total_tokens': 6700, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdfd-5a35-7f21-bcd2-a14b02fc7e01-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service otel-collector -n astronomy-shop -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-947df208aaa614a1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6047, 'output_tokens': 653, 'total_tokens': 6700}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    meta.helm.sh/release-name: astronomy-shop\n    meta.helm.sh/release-namespace: astronomy-shop\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-17T22:34:23Z"\n  labels:\n    app.kubernetes.io/component: agent-collector\n    app.kubernetes.io/instance: astronomy-shop\n    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: opentelemetry-collector\n    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: opentelemetry-collector\n    app.kubernetes.io/version: 0.142.0\n    component: agent-collector\n    helm.sh/chart: opentelemetry-collector-0.142.2\n  name: otel-collector\n  namespace: astronomy-shop\n  resourceVersion: "332192"\n  uid: c2e5cc12-94f2-4bf8-827d-c16bdca1cbc5\nspec:\n  clusterIP: 10.107.202.40\n  clusterIPs:\n  - 10.107.202.40\n  internalTrafficPolicy: Local\n  ipFamilies:\n  - IPv4\n  ipFamilyPolicy: SingleStack\n  ports:\n  - name: jaeger-compact\n    port: 6831\n    protocol: UDP\n    targetPort: 6831\n  - name: jaeger-grpc\n    port: 14250\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 14250\n  - name: jaeger-thrift\n    port: 14268\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 14268\n  - name: metrics\n    port: 8888\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 8888\n  - appProtocol: grpc\n    name: otlp\n    port: 4317\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 4317\n  - name: otlp-http\n    port: 4318\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 4318\n  - name: zipkin\n    port: 9411\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 9411\n  selector:\n    app.kubernetes.io/instance: astronomy-shop\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: opentelemetry-collector\n    component: agent-collector\n  sessionAffinity: None\n  type: ClusterIP\nstatus:\n  loadBalancer: {}\n', id='33f5056f-be4f-4070-be3e-1a8e563ccdcb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-947df208aaa614a1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5fdada70-6825-4630-9381-34e1e03871eb')]]
