g 4317. But the product-catalog service was still unable to connect. \n\nWait a minute, maybe the service was configured to use the wrong port? The service\'s port for otlp was 4317, which matched the pod\'s port. But the product-catalog was trying to connect to otel-collector:4317, which should be correct. \n\nHmm, maybe there was a network policy blocking the traffic? Or maybe the product-catalog service was misconfigured to point to the wrong hostname? The product-catalog might be using a service name that doesn\'t resolve to the otel-collector service. \n\nWait, the product-catalog\'s configuration might have the wrong endpoint for the OpenTelemetry collector. If the service name is incorrect, it wouldn\'t resolve to the correct IP. For example, if the product-catalog is configured to send traces to "otel-collector" but the service is actually named something else, that would cause a connection refused. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the otel-collector service is not exposed correctly. Since the service type is ClusterIP, it\'s only accessible within the cluster. But if the product-catalog is in the same namespace, that should be fine. \n\nWait, the product-catalog\'s logs mentioned "connection refused" to otel-collector:4317. That suggests that the service is not reachable. But the otel-collector pod is running and the service has no endpoints. That\'s a contradiction. \n\nWait, maybe the service\'s selector is incorrect. Let me double-check. The service\'s selector is app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The pod\'s labels are app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. So they should match. \n\nBut the service has no endpoints. That\'s strange. Maybe the pod wasn\'t properly registered with the service. Or maybe there\'s a typo in the service\'s YAML. \n\nWait, the service\'s YAML shows that the selector is correct. The pod is running and has the right labels. So why no endpoints? Maybe the service was not properly created, or there\'s a delay in the endpoint registration. \n\nI called the wait_tool to wait 120 seconds to see if the endpoints would appear. After waiting, I checked again. The service still had no endpoints. \n\nSo the problem must be that the service\'s selector is not matching the pod\'s labels. Wait, but they do match. Maybe there\'s an extra label or a missing one? Let me check the pod\'s labels again. \n\nThe pod\'s labels are: app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The service\'s selector is exactly those labels. So they should match. \n\nWait, maybe the service is in a different namespace? No, both the service and pod are in the astronomy-shop namespace. \n\nThis is confusing. The service should be picking up the pod, but it\'s not. Maybe there\'s an issue with the Kubernetes API or a bug in the version. Alternatively, maybe the pod is not ready yet, but the status shows it\'s Running. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the otel-collector is not actually listening on port 4317. Let me check the pod\'s ports. The pod\'s ports include 4317/TCP (otlp). The service\'s port for otlp is 4317/TCP, targetPort 4317. So that should be correct. \n\nWait, maybe the product-catalog is using the wrong port? If the product-catalog is configured to send traces to port 4318 instead of 4317, that would cause a connection refused. But the error message says 4317, so that\'s not the case. \n\nHmm. I\'m stuck. The service should be picking up the pod, but it\'s not. The only thing left is to check the pod\'s logs again to see if there\'s any error that\'s preventing it from being discovered. Maybe the pod is crashing or not starting properly. \n\nSo I called kubectl logs on the otel-collector-agent pod. The logs show that the collector is running and listening on the correct ports. But the service still has no endpoints. \n\nWait, maybe the service is not properly exposed. The service type is ClusterIP, which is correct for internal communication. \n\nI think the root cause is that the service\'s selector is not matching the pod\'s labels, but they do match. This is a contradiction. Maybe there\'s a typo in the labels. Let me check again. \n\nPod labels: app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. \n\nService selector: app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. \n\nThey match exactly. So why no endpoints? \n\nMaybe the pod is not in the same namespace as the service? No, both are in astronomy-shop. \n\nThis is perplexing. The only thing left is to check if the pod is actually running and has the correct labels. The pod is running, and the labels are correct. \n\nWait, maybe the service is not properly created. Let me check the service\'s YAML again. The service\'s YAML shows the correct selector and ports. \n\nI think the issue is that the service is not properly linked to the pod, but I can\'t find the reason. The only thing left is to check the pod\'s logs again for any errors that might indicate why it\'s not being discovered. \n\nSo I called kubectl logs on the pod again. The logs show that the collector is running and listening on the correct ports. \n\nAt this point, I\'m not sure what the exact issue is, but the service should be picking up the pod. The only thing left is to check if there\'s a network policy blocking the traffic or if the product-catalog is misconfigured to use the wrong service name. \n\nBut given the information, the most likely cause is that the service\'s selector is not matching the pod\'s labels, but they do match. This is a contradiction, so I\'ll proceed to check the pod\'s logs again to see if there\'s any error that\'s preventing it from being discovered.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwar