'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix an issue where the product-catalog service can\'t connect to the OpenTelemetry collector (otel-collector). The logs show connection refused errors on port 4317. \n\nFirst, I checked the pods and saw that the otel-collector-agent pod is running, but the service has no endpoints. That means the service isn\'t properly linked to the pod. The service\'s selector matches the pod\'s labels, so why no endpoints?\n\nWait, the service\'s selector is app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The pod has those same labels. So the selector should match. But the endpoints are still empty. Maybe there\'s a typo or a misconfiguration in the service\'s YAML?\n\nI checked the service\'s YAML and saw that the selector is correctly set. The pod is running and has the right labels. Hmm. Maybe the service isn\'t properly created? Or maybe there\'s a delay in the endpoint registration. \n\nWait, the pod\'s logs show that the OTLP receiver is starting on 10.244.0.254:4317. The service\'s IP is 10.107.202.40, but the pod\'s IP is 10.244.0.254. The service might not be correctly pointing to the pod\'s IP. Or maybe the service is in the wrong namespace? No, both are in astronomy-shop.\n\nWait, the service\'s type is ClusterIP, which should route traffic to the pods. But if the service\'s selector isn\'t matching any pods, endpoints won\'t be created. But the pod does match the selector. Maybe the service was deleted and not recreated properly? Or there\'s a version mismatch?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the otel-collector-agent pod isn\'t actually exposing the port 4317. Let me check the pod\'s ports. The pod\'s ports include 4317/TCP (otlp). So that\'s correct. \n\nWait, the service\'s ports include otlp:4317/TCP, which matches the pod\'s port. So why no endpoints? Maybe the service is not properly associated with the pod\'s IP. Or maybe the pod isn\'t ready yet? But the pod has been running for a while.\n\nWait, the service\'s endpoints are <none>, but the pod is running. Maybe the service is not properly configured to use the correct labels. Or maybe there\'s a typo in the service\'s selector. Let me double-check the service\'s YAML again. The selector is app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The pod\'s labels are the same. So that\'s correct.\n\nHmm. Maybe the issue is that the service is not properly created. Let me try to describe the service again. Wait, the user already did that. The service has no endpoints. \n\nWait, maybe the otel-collector-agent pod is not part of the service\'s selector. Let me check the pod\'s labels again. The pod has app.kubernetes.io/instance=astronomy-shop, app.kubernetes.io/name=opentelemetry-collector, component=agent-collector. The service\'s selector includes all three. So it should match. \n\nI\'m confused. The service should have endpoints, but it doesn\'t. Maybe there\'s a problem with the service\'s configuration that\'s not visible. Alternatively, maybe the service is not properly created, and the user needs to recreate it. \n\nWait, the user might need to check if the service is correctly associated with the pod. Since the service\'s selector matches the pod\'s labels, but no endpoints are present, perhaps the service is not properly created. Maybe the user should delete and recreate the service. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is in a different namespace. But both are in astronomy-shop. \n\nWait, the product-catalog service is trying to connect to otel-collector:4317. If the otel-collector service has no endpoints, the DNS lookup fails, leading to connection refused. So the root cause is the otel-collector service not having endpoints, which is due to the service not being properly linked to the pod. \n\nThe solution would be to ensure the service\'s selector matches the pod\'s labels. Since it does, maybe the service needs to be recreated. Or there\'s a typo in the service\'s YAML. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the pod is not properly labeled. Let me check the pod\'s labels again. The pod has the correct labels. \n\nI think the issue is that the service is not properly configured, even though the labels match. Maybe the service needs to be updated. The user should check the service\'s YAML again and ensure that the selector is correct. If it is, then the service should have endpoints. \n\nBut since the service has no endpoints, the user might need to delete the service and recreate it. Alternatively, check if the pod is actually running and has the correct labels. \n\nWait, the pod is running and has the correct labels. So the service should have endpoints. But it doesn\'t. Maybe there\'s a bug in the Kubernetes setup. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not properly associated with the pod because of a different reason. \n\nIn any case, the root cause is that the otel-collector service has no endpoints, leading to connection issues. The solution is to ensure the service\'s selector matches the pod\'s labels and that the service is properly created. If that\'s not the case, the service needs to be recreated.\n</think>\n\nThe `otel-collector` service has no endpoints despite the `otel-collector-agent` pod having matching labels and being in the correct namespace. This indicates a misconfiguration or a failure in endpoint registration. The logs show the pod is running and the service\'s selector matches the pod\'s labels, but the service still has no endpoints. This could be due to a typo in the service\'s YAML, a delay in endpoint registration, or a misconfiguration in the service\'s selector. To resolve this, I will update the service\'s YAML to explicitly ensure the selector matches the pod\'s labels and recreate the service to force endpoint registration.\n\n**Faulty Component:** The `otel-collector` service is misconfigured or the pod\'s labels do not match the service\'s selector, leading to no endpoints and connection failures.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl apply -f - <<EOF\\napiVersion: v1\\nkind: Service\\nmetadata:\\n  name: otel-collector\\n  namespace: astronomy-shop\\n  labels:\\n    app.kubernetes.io/component: agent-collector\\n    app.kubernetes.io/instance: astronomy-shop\\n    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm\\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: opentelemetry-collector\\n    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: opentelemetry-collector\\n    app.kubernetes.io/version: 0.142.0\\n    component: agent-collector\\n    helm.sh/chart: opentelemetry-collector-0.142.2\\nspec:\\n  clusterIP: 10.107.202.40\\n  internalTrafficPolicy: Local\\n  ipFamilies:\\n  - IPv4\\n  ipFamilyPolicy: SingleStack\\n  ports:\\n  - name: jaeger-compact\\n    port: 6831\\n    protocol: UDP\\n    targetPort: 6831\\n  - name: jaeger-grpc\\n    port: 14250\\n    protocol: TCP\\n    targetPort: 14250\\n  - name: jaeger-thrift\\n    port: 14268\\n    protocol: TCP\\n    targetPort: 14268\\n  - name: metrics\\n    port: 8888\\n    protocol: TCP\\n    targetPort: 8888\\n  - name: otlp\\n    port: 4317\\n    protocol: TCP\\n    targetPort: 4317\\n  - name: otlp-http\\n    port: 4318\\n    protocol: TCP\\n    targetPort: 4318\\n  - name: zipkin\\n    port: 9411\\n    protocol: TCP\\n    targetPort: 9411\\n  selector:\\n    app.kubernetes.io/instance: astronomy-shop\\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: opentelemetry-collector\\n    component: agent-collector\\n  sessionAffinity: None\\n  type: ClusterIP\\nEOF"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1822, 'prompt_tokens': 10085, 'total_tokens': 11907, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe00-6c14-7381-9b9c-c05615d6e9d5-0', tool_ca